From 174b1409ea816f44b02504ec1f9fcd1f22dbfb06 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 17:45:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 90/90] minicargo: infer binaries from src/bin/ as cargo does

tuc 1.3.0 (GitHub source) has `src/lib.rs` and `src/bin/tuc.rs`, and no
`[[bin]]` in its Cargo.toml. minicargo built the library, reported 100%, and
produced no executable, so `destroot` failed. Only `src/main.rs` was ever
inferred as a binary. (The crates.io copy of tuc works, because cargo's
normalised manifest spells out the `[[bin]]`.)

The inferred binaries are now the ones cargo infers (toml_targets.rs):
`src/main.rs` named after the package, `src/bin/<name>.rs` and
`src/bin/<name>/main.rs`. They're combined with explicit `[[bin]]`s as cargo
does:
- With no `[[bin]]`, all inferred binaries are built (unless `autobins = false`).
- With `[[bin]]`s, the inferred ones are added too from edition 2018 on
  (cargo only warns about them in 2015). One with the name or path of
  a `[[bin]]` is that same binary.
- A `[[bin]]` with no `path` takes the path of the inferred binary with its
  name. `[[bin]] name = "<package>"` next to `src/main.rs` used to point at
  a nonexistent `src/bin/<package>/main.rs`.

An implicit `src/main.rs` keeps minicargo's existing output name (`foo_bar`
for package `foo-bar`).

`list_directory` moved from main.cpp (workspace member globs) to
`os_support`, so manifest.cpp can use it too.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
